<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis the Pious</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/778">778</a>–<a href="/years/840">840 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis the Pious was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> from 814 to 840. His attempts to arrange the succession among his sons provoked rebellions and civil wars. After his death the empire was divided among his sons by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious.jpg" alt="Louis the Pious" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwik_I_Pobo%C5%BCny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Avars are last mentioned in 822, when their envoys appeared at an assembly of <b>Louis the Pious</b> at Frankfurt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Two of Charlemagne's three legitimate adult sons, Charles the Younger and Pepin of Italy, died before him, which allowed <b>Louis the Pious</b> to inherit the undivided empire in 814 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne had created a sub-kingdom of Aquitaine for his son <b>Louis the Pious</b> in 781, but by the late ninth century real power there had passed to great regional nobles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg takes its name from the Hammaburg, a fortification built in the early ninth century near the meeting of the Alster and the Elbe, probably under Charlemagne or <b>Louis the Pious</b>, to secure the Frankish frontier in Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Royal Frankish Annals record “leaders of the Slavs who live along the Danube” at <b>Louis the Pious</b>'s court in 811 and embassies of Moravians at Frankfurt in 822, the earliest certain reference to them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne and <b>Louis the Pious</b> ruled through assemblies, written ordinances called capitularies, travelling royal agents called missi dominici and the network of counts, and tried to bind the realm together through a common Christian discipline, set out in the Admonitio generalis of 789.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chasseneuil-du-poitou">Chasseneuil-du-Poitou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingelheim-am-rhein">Ingelheim am Rhein</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.65,0.37&amp;year=818" aria-label="Chasseneuil-du-Poitou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4559 524 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis the Pious"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5009 794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5009 794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.65,0.37&amp;year=818">What was here in 818 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
